    "processes": ["derivation", "sound-change"],
    "headline": "Omonzuru shifted from Classical Japanese 重んず (omonzu), from 重 (omo, stem of 重い (omoi, “heavy”)) plus み plus す, literally “to treat as heavy”.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "Regular shift in conjugation from Classical Japanese verb 重んず (omonzu), a phonetic shift from Classical Japanese 重みす (omomi su), from 重 (omo, stem of adjective 重い (omoi, “heavy”)) + み (-mi) + す (su, “to do”), where みす (-mi su) was a grammaticalised pattern meaning “treat (noun) as (adjective); find (noun) (adjective)”. Literally, “to treat as heavy”. Compare the kango (漢語) 重視 (jūshi, “value, esteem, respect”), from 重 (“heavy; important”) + 視 (“to see”)."
      }
    ],
    "wiktionaryDump": "2026-09-02"
  },
